Good — this is actually very helpful. I can see exactly what broke now.

Your Node upgrade failed because of a package conflict with libnode-dev, which is why npm and npx disappeared and Node stayed at v12.22.9. That error is clearly shown here:


So we need to fix Node installation first, then build the Directus extension properly. Nothing else will work until Node/npm/npx work.

Fix Node Installation (this is the blocker)

Run these commands exactly in this order:

sudo apt remove -y nodejs libnode-dev npm
sudo apt autoremove -y
sudo apt clean

Then install Node 22 again:

curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_22.x | sudo -E bash -
sudo apt install -y nodejs

Now verify:

node -v
npm -v
npx -v

You should see something like:

v22.x.x
10.x.x
10.x.x

If that works, we are finally in business.

Then create the Directus extension correctly

After Node works:

cd ~/directus
mkdir extension-dev
cd extension-dev

npx create-directus-extension@latest

Choose:

type: endpoint
name: test-endpoint
language: javascript

Then build it:

cd directus-extension-test-endpoint
npm install
npm run build

After build, you should see:

dist/index.js
package.json
Move built extension into Directus extensions folder

Back to your main Directus folder:

cd ~/directus
rm -rf extensions/*
cp -r extension-dev/directus-extension-test-endpoint extensions/

Your structure should now be:

directus/
  extensions/
    directus-extension-test-endpoint/
      package.json
      dist/
        index.js
Restart Directus
docker compose down
docker compose up -d
docker logs -f directus-directus-1

You should see something like:

Loaded extension: directus-extension-test-endpoint
Test endpoint

Open in browser:

http://YOUR_SERVER_IP:8055/directus-extension-test-endpoint

or sometimes:

http://YOUR_SERVER_IP:8055/test-endpoint

(depending on extension config)

